@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Sections

@* The block every screen opens with: what the page is called, what stands under that, and whatever
   sits at the header's other end. The same divs used to be redrawn on every page - which is why the
   "shared by" line above a note read differently from the one above an event, and why a subtitle sat
   beside its title on three pages and stacked under it everywhere else. One component now draws the
   header; a page only says what it has.

   The heading is a row only where there is a leading button to sit beside the title - see Notes.razor,
   Tasks.razor, Calendar.razor and Inventories.razor, the four screens that open with "add another of
   these". Everywhere else the title and whatever stands under it simply stack, the way block elements
   always have where nothing tried to lay them out side by side. *@
<div class="page-header">
    <div class="@(LeadingAction is null ? null : "page-heading")">
        @LeadingAction
        @* Inside the heading rather than after it, so the mark reads as part of the title's own line -
           the same place a field's "?" takes beside its label. *@
        <h1>@Title@if (Description is not null)
            {
                <FieldHint>@Description</FieldHint>
            }</h1>
        @if (Subtitle is not null)
        {
            <p class="page-subtitle">@Subtitle</p>
        }
        @if (Hint is not null)
        {
            <span class="form-hint">@Hint</span>
        }
    </div>
    @* Its own slot rather than a share of the one below: a phone's Menu button arrives through a section
       rather than through a parameter (see PageToolbarTrigger), so this has to be here whether or not the
       page has actions of its own - and the promise that a page with no actions draws no empty row there
       is about that row, not this one. Empty, this collapses to nothing and is hidden outright. *@
    <div class="page-header-toolbar"><SectionOutlet SectionName="@PageToolbarTrigger.SectionName" /></div>
    @if (Actions is not null)
    {
        <div class="page-header-actions">@Actions</div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The one control that makes another of whatever the page lists, drawn before the title. The only
    /// thing that turns the heading into a row - a page with nothing here gets the title and whatever is
    /// under it stacked instead.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? LeadingAction { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RenderFragment? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the page is for, folded behind a "?" beside the title rather than stated under it - see
    /// FieldHint.razor. Every screen used to open with a sentence explaining itself, and a sentence read
    /// once and then scrolled past on every visit after is the same small print a form's fields gave up.
    /// The words are unchanged; they are one press or one hover away.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Description { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whatever a page states under its title about the thing it shows - a "Shared by" line, a count of
    /// what a group list gathers, a storage's own description. Not what the page is for: that is
    /// <see cref="Description"/>, and this is what stays in view because it changes with the object.
    /// Left as the page's own markup rather than fixed text, since what it says differs by object;
    /// wrapped here so every page's version reads the same.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Subtitle { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The one page that has nothing to save yet and says so beside its title instead of under it - see
    /// NoteEditor.razor. Kept apart from <see cref="Subtitle"/> because it is a different sentence in a
    /// different place, not a second copy of the same one.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Hint { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whatever sits at the header's other end - a sort menu, a back button, a Clear. Left out entirely,
    /// rather than drawn empty, when a page has nothing there.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Actions { get; set; }
}
